WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.770
A weather app showed minus thirty

2
00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.450
millimetres of rain at our school.

3
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.480
Someone had typed a minus sign.

4
00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.190
It reached three other apps

5
00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.690
before anyone noticed.

6
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.130
Today: data quality tests, contracts,

7
00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.630
and what to do when a test fails.

8
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.660
Quality has several sides.

9
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.890
Valid: the right type and range.

10
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.550
Complete: nothing missing.

11
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.380
Unique: no duplicates.

12
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.070
Consistent: the tables agree.

13
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.950
Timely: it arrived on time.

14
00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.450
Write each test as code that

15
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.910
runs on every batch.

16
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.520
In SQL, a test is a query that

17
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.340
should return no rows: orphans,

18
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.700
duplicates, values out of range.

19
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.990
A data contract is an agreement

20
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.110
between the team that produces data

21
00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.970
and the teams that use it.

22
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.590
It names the columns, types and allowed

23
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:03.810
values, how fresh the data must be, and

24
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.010
who to call when it breaks.

25
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:06.890
Quick check!

26
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.340
Which test catches a mark for pupil

27
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.180
seven, when there is no pupil seven?

28
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.890
Referential integrity: every foreign key

29
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.050
must point to a row that exists.

30
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.190
A left join that finds NULL on the

31
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.190
other side finds the orphans.

32
00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:29.070
Then decide what a failure does.

33
00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:31.560
Block the whole load when

34
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.520
the problem is serious.

35
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.290
Quarantine the bad rows when

36
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.630
most of the batch is fine.

37
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:39.370
Or alert and let it through,

38
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.970
when a late report costs more

39
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.310
than a slightly wrong one.

40
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.240
In Python, check each row against the

41
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:50.340
contract: is the mark a number, and in

42
00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:50.600
range?

43
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.660
Collect the failures with their reasons,

44
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:56.340
so the producer can fix them at the

45
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:56.580
source.

46
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.440
In the app, four weather clubs send

47
00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.460
daily batches with planted faults.

48
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.340
Choose the checks and the action,

49
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.600
and watch the rainfall report.

50
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:11.200
Notice which faults no check catches.

51
00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:13.080
Your turn.

52
00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:15.690
A test fails on three rows

53
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.670
out of ten thousand.

54
00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:19.870
Which response keeps the pipeline

55
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.470
moving and the data honest?

56
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.760
Quarantine them and alert the owner, so

57
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.420
good data flows and the bad gets fixed.

58
00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.350
Remember: test quality as

59
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.990
code, on every batch.

60
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.140
Agree a contract with

61
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.540
whoever produces the data.

62
00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.100
And choose block, quarantine or

63
00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.800
alert by what each failure costs.

64
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.490
Try it tomorrow: write a data contract

65
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:48.810
for a dataset you use, like your class

66
00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.730
timetable or a club's results.

67
00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.850
List the columns, types and allowed

68
00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:56.490
values, and who fixes it when it's wrong.
